<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Demajagua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1868</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Demajagua was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> estate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-manuel-de-cespedes">Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</a> near Manzanillo in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. There, on 10 October 1868, Céspedes freed his slaves and proclaimed Cuban independence in the Grito de Yara, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a>. The site, damaged by Spanish shelling, is now a national monument, and its bell is a symbol of Cuban independence.</p></div>
